natively, maybe forming a Seven Pairs. But I need seven pairs. Let's see: I have two B1s, but others are singles. That's not enough. \n\nLooking at the points, if I can form All Chows, which is worth 24 points. Wait, All Chows is four chows and a pair. Let's see: I have one meld (B4-B5-B6), and if I can form three more chows and a pair. Let's see:\n\n- B4-B5-B6 (Chow)\n- T5-T6-T7 (Chow)\n- W7-W8-W9 (if I can get W9)\n- T2-T3-T4 (if I can get T4)\n- Pair of B1 or B9?\n\nBut I don't have W9 or T4. So this seems unlikely. \n\nAnother thought: Maybe forming a Full Flush by discarding the Characters. I have W7 and W8. If I can discard them, then my hand would be all in Bots and Dots. But I also have T2, T3, T5, T6, T7, T9. So that's a mix of Bots and Dots. Not a single suit. So Full Flush isn't possible here.\n\nWhat about a Pure Straight? That requires three chows in the same suit. But my meld is in Bots, and the other chows are in Dots or other suits. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe forming a Chicken Hand. But that's only if the hand is worth zero points, which might not be the case here.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options are to play B1, T6, T9, T2, T7, W7, W8, B9, T3, T5. \n\nIf I play B1, I have another B1 left. That might be a pair. If I can form a pair of B1s, and then have chows. Let's see: If I play one B1, then I have one B1 left. But I need a pair for the All Chows. If I keep the B1 as a pair, that could help. But I have to see if I can form four chows.\n\nAlternatively, playing T6. If I play T6, then I have T5 and T7 left. Maybe forming a chow with T5-T6-T7 if I can keep T6. Wait, I already have a meld of B4-B5-B6. If I play T6, then I can't form that chow. But I have T5, T6, T7. If I keep them, that's a chow. So maybe playing T6 is not a good idea if I want to form a chow.\n\nWait, I already have a meld of B4-B5-B6. So that's one chow. If I can form another chow with T5-T6-T7, that's another chow. Then, I need two more chows and a pair. Let's see