iles. If the user discards W7, they have W8 and W9. If they discard W9, they have W7 and W8. Either way, they can't form the chow. But which is better?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should discard a tile that is less needed. For example, W8 is part of the middle of the chow. If they discard it, they can't form the chow. But if they discard W7 or W9, they might still have a chance to form a pair with the remaining two. For example, if they discard W7, they have W8 and W9. If they draw a W7, they can form a chow. But if they discard W8, they can't form the chow unless they get W8 again. However, the user has to make a decision now.\n\nConsidering the unshown tiles, W8 has 3 left, W7 has 3, W9 has 4. So W8 is less available. Discarding W8 might be riskier. So maybe discard W9, which has 4 left. If they discard W9, there are still four W9s left, so the chance of getting it back is higher. But if they need the chow, they need all three.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is better off discarding a tile from the T7-T8-T9 chow. T9 has only 1 left. If they discard T9, it's more likely to be taken by others. So better to keep T9 and discard a W tile.\n\nGiven all this, the best move might be to discard a W tile. Between W7, W8, W9. Since W8 is in the middle, discarding it would split the chow. But if the user discards W8, they can still form a pair with W7 and W9 if they have both. Wait, no. A pair requires two of the same tile. So if they discard W8, they have W7 and W9 left, which are different tiles. No pair. So that's not helpful.\n\nTherefore, the user needs to discard a tile that doesn't block a potential chow and allows for a pair. The only way to have a pair is to have two of the same tile. The user currently has no pairs. If they discard a tile, they need to have a pair in the remaining tiles. The only way this is possible is if they have two of the same tile in the remaining seven tiles. But the user has one of each tile. So unless they draw a tile, they can't form a pair. This suggests that the user's hand is not structured to win with the current tiles, and they are waiting for a specific tile to complete a pair or a chow.\n\nGiven that, the best strategy is to discard a tile that is least likely to be needed for forming a chow or a pair. Since the T2 and T3 are part of a potential chow with T4, which is still available (unshown T4: 2), it's better to keep both T2 and T3. So the user should avoid discarding T2 or T3. The remaining options are T9, T7, W7, W9, W8, T8.\n\nAmong these, discarding T9 would block the T7-T8-T9 chow. T9 has only 1 left, so it's more likely to be taken. Discarding T7 would block the T7-T8-T9 chow. W7, W8, W9 block their respective chows. T8 blocks the T7-T8-T9 chow.\n\nThe best option might be to discard a tile that is part of a chow with more available tiles. For example, W9 has 4 left, so discarding W9 might be safer. Alternatively, T9 has 1 left, so discarding T9 is riskier.\n\nIf the user discards W9, they have W7 and W8 left. The unshown W9s are 4, so there's a good chance to draw it. If they draw W9, they can form the W7-W8-W9 chow. Similarly, if they discard T9, they have T7 and T8, and the unshown T9 is 1, so less chance to draw it.\n\nTherefore, discarding W9 is better because it's more likely to be drawn again. However, the user needs to form four chows and a pair. If they discard W9, they can still form the W7-W8-W9 chow if they draw W9. But they also need a pair. Without a pair, the hand isn't valid. So maybe the user is waiting for a tile to complete a pair.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is aiming for a different pattern. For example, Seven Pairs. But they need seven pairs. The current hand has no pairs. If they discard a tile, they still don't have pairs. So that's not likely.\n\nAnother possibility: the user has a concealed hand. If they discard a tile, they can still have a concealed hand. But the current melds are two chows, which are exposed. So the concealed hand would be the remaining tiles. But the user needs to form four melds and a pair. If the remaining tiles are concealed, but the existing melds are exposed, it's a mixed hand.\n\nGiven all this, the best move is to discard a tile that is part of a chow with more available tiles, to increase the chance of drawing it. So discard W9. That way, if they draw W9, they can complete the W7-W8-W9 chow. Also, the T7-T8-T9 chow can be completed if they keep T9. So the user has two potential chows to complete: W7-8-9 and T7-8-9. Discarding W9 leaves T9 in hand, so they can still form the T7-T8-T9 chow if they have T9. Therefore, the best move is to discard W9.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand is structured with two existing chows and potential for two more chows (W7-W8-W9 and T7-T8-T9). Discarding W9 preserves the possibility of completing the T7-T8-T9 chow while retaining more available W9 tiles for future draws.  \nReason: W9 has 4 unshown tiles, making it more likely to be drawn later. Discarding it maintains the T7-T8-T9 chow potential and avoids blocking critical chows.  \nAnswer: Play W9"}
